# Using the Debugger

Gamma Debugger, or the Debugger for short, is the interactive
companion to Gamma.  You can use it to inspect the geometry from
multiple program outputs inside one or more windows, which can be
partitioned arbitrarily into viewports.  When you make changes to your
program, it can invoke Gamma for you and refresh the displayed
outputs, collectively or separately.  It is operated by commands which
you can type at the terminal, if you prefer to use it as a
command-driven debugger.  Alternatively, you can bind commands to
keystokes and use the Debugger exclusively through the viewing
windows, for an interface similar to an Integrated Development
Environment (IDE).

Concept: debugger

## Invoking the Debugger from the Command Line

You can start the Debugger by running the program c`gammadb`, possibly
followed by one or more options. Once started, it reads commands from
the terminal until you tell it to exit, which you can do by typing the
c`quit` or c`exit` commands (they are equivalent), or by pressing
k`Ctrl-D` when the command line is empty.

Concept: debugger,initialization file

During startup, the Debugger looks in the current working directory
for a file named f`.gammadbinit`.  This is called an
def:{initialization file}.  If it exists, lines are read from it and
executed as commands, exactly as if they had been typed at the prompt.
The initialization file can contain any of the commands described in
ref: Debugger Commands.  It may additionally contain comments, which
start with a s`#` anywhere on a line and extend until the end of the
line.  They are ignored.  Blank lines are also allowed and are
likewise ignored.

You can place an initialization file in the same directory as your
program and then simply invoke c`gammadb` from this directory, without
need of any further arguments.  Here are some things you can do with
an initialization file:

  1. Set up Debugger settings.  At a minimum, you should set `args` to
     tell the Debugger how to run your program, but there are other
     useful settings.  Ref:{Commands for Settings} for a complete
     list.
  2. Create and configure one or more windows to view your program's
     output.  Every window can be partitioned arbitrarily into
     viewports, each of which is dedicated to displaying the geometry
     of a single output.  Ref:{Window Commands} for details on how to
     create windows and ref:{Viewport Commands} on how to set them up.
  3. Define key bindings, so that key presses while the mouse cursor
     is inside one of the viewing windows execute specific commands.
     You can use such key bindings to tell the Debugger to re-run your
     program and update its viewports, toggle viewport state, such as
     whether vertices, edges or faces are drawn, or temporarily
     maximize a vieport for easier inspection of its geometry.
     Ref:{Binding Commands} for details.

You may want to end your initialization file with a c`run` or
c`present` command for each window.  These ensure the window is made
visible and if not present in the initialization file will need to be
typed at the prompt on each startup.  Once a viewing window is
visible, further interaction can then proceed by means of key
bindings. Ref:{A Few Examples} for a practical example of a complete
initialization file.

## Typing Commands Interactively

Concept: debugger,commands

The Debugger uses the GNU Readline library, so when you need to type
commands interactively, you can benefit from its extensive set of
command-line editing and history features.  These are documented in
Readline's manual, so this section will only describe the aspects that
are particular to the Debugger.

### Command History

Readline keeps track of the commands you type and they can be
recalled, to be edited or re-executed.  By default the 100 last
commands are retained in the command history, but you can change that
limit if needed, by means of the `history-size` setting.  Consecutive
identical commands are included in the history only once.  Blank lines
are ignored.

When the `save-history` setting is enabled, the Debugger writes the
command history to a file named f`.gammadb_history` in the current
directory on exit.  The history is then automatically recovered from
this file on the next startup.  If the setting is disabled (this is
the default), command history is discarded when you quit.

You can configure these settings in your initialization file.  Ref:
Commands for Settings, for details.

### Command Completion

The Debugger also provides context-sensitive command completion.  You
can use it to type only part of a keyword and let Readline fill in the
rest for you.  The default completion key is k`Tab`.

The Debugger knows what part of a command you're typing and completes
accordingly.  For example, if you type s`sp` right after the prompt
and press k`Tab`, it will be completed to s`split`, with a space
character helpfully appended so that you can immediately continue
typing in the first argument.  If you do so by typing s`h` and
pressing k`Tab` again, it is completed to s`horizontally`, which is
one of the two arguments expected by the `split` command.  Had you
tried to complete s`h` right after the prompt, it would have resulted
in the s`hide` command instead.

When there are more than one possible candidates, only their common
prefix will be completed when you press k`Tab`.  Completion cannot
continue after that, but you can press k`Tab` twice to display the
list of candidates.  You can then consult it and type in more text so
as to make the completionn unambiguous, before pressing k`Tab` again.

For example, if you type s`p` at the start of a command and press
k`Tab`, it will be completed only up to s`pr`.  Pressing k`Tab` two
more times, will list the two candidates, which are the commands
s`present` and s`print`.  You need only add s`e`, before pressing
k`Tab` again to get to s`present`.

Because the suggested completions are sensitive to the command you
have typed in so far, this feature can be helpful in more than one
ways.  For instance you can type s`set`, or s`show` and press k`Tab`
twice to get a list of all available settings Doing the same with
s`bind` instead of s`set` will get you a list of all keys you can
bind.

